trace.content:
[THOUGHT] We have a confirmed diagnosis of well-differentiated endometrial adenocarcinoma, a persistent left adnexal mass, and no conclusive imaging or lab results despite multiple attempts. All high-yield non-invasive tests (CA-125, HE4, MRI, CT, targeted TVUS) have returned “Not specified” — indicating they are either unavailable or unreportable in this environment. At 15/16 turns used, efficiency is critical. The single most definitive, guideline-recommended, and *available* next step — per ACOG and ESMO — is **hysteroscopy with directed biopsy of any visible endometrial lesion**, even after a positive endometrial biopsy. Why? Because:  
- Endometrial biopsy has ~90% sensitivity but can miss focal lesions (e.g., polyps, early carcinoma in a background of atrophy);  
- Hysteroscopy allows direct visualization to confirm the extent and morphology of the endometrial lesion, assess for cervical canal involvement, and rule out synchronous endocervical malignancy;  
- It may also reveal whether the adnexal mass is associated with tubal pathology (e.g., hydrosalpinx, fimbrial tumor) — though limited, it adds discriminative anatomic context;  
- And critically: hysteroscopy is widely available, minimally invasive, and often performed preoperatively in endometrial cancer workup.  

This is the last high-value, atomic, available test before exhausting turns — and it directly addresses diagnostic certainty and surgical planning.

[TEST] Hysteroscopy with directed endometrial biopsy

